WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.512 --> 00:00:01.970
DEREK DOWNEY: Hi,
I'm Derek Downey,

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.960
a Developer Advocate at Google.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.020
I am currently working
on developing a banking

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.960
application that leverages
Cloud Spanner for its ability

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.120
to seamlessly scale
without losing

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.640
strong external consistency.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.010
In my last video, I
highlighted how simple

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.140
it was to get started
working with the Spanner

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.540
Emulator running
on my own device

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.750
to run my basic banking
app that's in development.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:26.700
Now, I want to connect to
a real Spanner instance

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.120
so it can be used for
development by my team.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.680
In this video, I will dig deeper
into what a Spanner instance is

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.630
and the different
options available

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:37.070
when creating an instance.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.060
Like all other Google
Cloud products,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.480
Spanner instances must be
created inside a project.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.480
So my Spanner instance will be
created in my organization's

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.610
development project.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.880
For Spanner, an instance
refers to an amount

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.330
of compute and storage capacity
that you get access to.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.730
An instance will have
at least three replicas

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.120
created, depending on which
configuration you choose.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.540
This means that Spanner provides
high availability by default.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.970
Each instance can have
multiple databases,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.410
depending on your needs.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.740
For my banking application, I
will create a single database

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.360
for customers and
their transaction.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.340
The cost of this
instance is determined

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.790
by how much compute capacity
I provision, how much storage

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.480
my database uses, how much
storage my backups use,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.110
and finally, how much
network bandwidth I use.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.090
As I get started on
this application,

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:37.440
the largest cost will come from
the compute capacity itself.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.570
The compute capacity
cost is determined

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.930
by both the number of
nodes and the configuration

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.520
that I choose.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.160
I should mention that
in Spanner, nodes

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.560
is the measurement of how
much compute capacity that

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.360
is provisioned for my instance.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.510
This setting has no bearing
on how many replicas I get.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:58.760
I will always get at
least three replicas.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.430
Since I am just getting
started with this application,

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:04.880
a full node of compute
capacity is too much.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.190
Luckily, I can go smaller
than a single node

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.919
by using processing
units instead.

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:13.580
1,000 processing units is the
equivalent compute capacity

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.410
of a single node in Spanner.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.310
And I can provision these
in increments of 100.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:23.130
So I will create my instance
with 100 processing units,

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:25.460
which gives me roughly
1/10 the compute

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:29.870
resources of a single
node at 1/10 the cost.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.450
Besides sizing, the
other important factor

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.750
for creating a Spanner
instance is the configuration.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.810
The configuration determines
how many replicas I get

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.090
and where they are located.

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:43.030
It also determines
the type of replicas

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.920
that are handled by Spanner.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.110
And luckily, as a
developer, I don't

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:48.970
have to manage the replicas.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.700
They are completely
managed by Spanner.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.040
There are two types of
configurations in Spanner--

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.560
regional and multi-regional.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.630
For regional
configurations, I would

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:03.380
get three read-write replicas
in the region I choose.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.710
Read-write replicas participate
in votes to commit rights,

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:09.560
and these replicas can
also be promoted to leader

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.970
for high availability.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:13.220
A regional configuration
is a good option

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.140
if my application
will be operating

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.060
within a single region.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.090
I want to choose a region
where my application is located

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.160
to reduce request latency.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.170
To achieve higher
availability, though, I

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.570
would consider a
multi-regional configuration.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.300
These configurations
provide read-write replicas

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.780
in two regions, as well as
maintaining a witness replica

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:33.980
in a third region.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.380
Witness replicas do
not contain any data,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.930
but they do provide
votes for quorum.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.650
Also, witness replicas can never
be promoted to become a leader.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.950
Some configurations also
offer read-only replicas

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:47.240
in additional regions.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.580
These replicas contain
a full copy of data

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.770
so that they can reduce read
latencies in that region.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.130
However, they can never
be promoted as a leader,

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:57.500
and read-only replicas
don't participate

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.030
in voting to commit rights.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.940
So they don't impact
write latency performance.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.520
Multi-regional configurations
are a great way

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:06.320
to achieve the
highest availability

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.450
for your Spanner-backed
applications.

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.000
It's important that
applications are effectively

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.010
deployed in the
appropriate regions

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.310
to take full advantage
of the configuration.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.769
You can get more information
on available Spanner

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:21.709
configurations from the
link in the description.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:23.450
For my development
application, I'm

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:28.710
going to create a regional
configuration in US Central 1.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.700
All right, so to
create my instance,

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.600
I provided a size
and a configuration.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.880
All I need now is
to provide the name

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.640
and an optional description
for the instance.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.990
Then I can create it.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.120
Once the instance
is created, I will

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.960
need to update my
application to use it.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.050
To connect to Spanner, I
will need the project ID

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.050
for the project that my
Spanner instance is located in.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:56.010
I will also need
the instance ID.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.050
I also need to
authenticate my machine

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.900
to be able to work with
the project resources.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.780
I do this using
the gcloud command.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:08.110
Now, in Intellij, I will
create a new data source

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:11.060
and point my new Spanner
instance using the project

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.970
and instance information.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.580
The URL connection
string is different,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:19.120
as it no longer points
to the Emulator.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.570
And there-- it's connected.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.420
Now I will need to
modify the application

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.040
code with the project
and instance information.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.590
If I run my commands
to create the database,

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:32.210
it will create
the customer table

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.960
on the development Spanner
instance that I just created.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.750
Now, we have our
banking application

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.050
using a real Spanner instance
instead of the Emulator.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:44.200
My team can start
using it today.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.480
My instance is a
regional configuration

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.330
with a small amount of compute
resources for our development

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.170
needs.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:52.610
Choosing the right
configuration is important.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:54.670
So I have provided
additional resources

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.550
on Spanner configurations
in the video description.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:59.410
These resources
can help you decide

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:03.220
which configuration might be the
right one for your application.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:05.050
In our next video,
I will dig deeper

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:08.650
into what database
options exist for Spanner.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.360
In the meantime,
please let us know

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.790
if you have any questions
about Spanner instances,

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:14.290
costs, or configurations.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:18.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

